Subject: nv30: a fragment program loop ends on an instruction slot

nv40_fp_rep_end set the REP instruction's end offset to the last instruction's
offset + 4. That is the slot after the loop only while the last instruction has
no constant of its own; with one, its 16-byte constant follows it in the
program, and the offset named that data slot. The end is now the program's
length so far, the next instruction slot (the appended NOP and END when the loop
is the last thing in the program). Programs whose loops end on an instruction
without a constant are unchanged.

Forwarded: no

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nvfx_fragprog.c b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nvfx_fragprog.c
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nvfx_fragprog.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nvfx_fragprog.c
@@ -330,7 +330,10 @@ nv40_fp_rep_end(struct nvfx_fpc *fpc)
 {
    unsigned rep = util_dynarray_pop(&fpc->loop_stack, unsigned);
    uint32_t *hw = &fpc->fp->insn[rep];
-   hw[3] = fpc->inst_offset + 4;
+   /* the loop ends at the next instruction slot. The last instruction's offset + 4 is that slot
+    * only while it has no constant of its own: with one, it is the constant's slot, which is
+    * data and not somewhere the program can continue (the RSX's driver refuses such a loop) */
+   hw[3] = fpc->fp->insn_len;
 }
 
 #if 0
